      causes: [
        "Count I — Writ of Mandate (CCP §1085): Failure to Perform Mandatory Duty Under Penal Code §679.10(b) — compel certification of Form I-918 Supplement B",
        "Count II — Writ of Mandate (CCP §1085): Failure to Perform Ministerial Duty Under PC §679.10(g) — reason-giving requirement for denials",
        "Count III — Writ of Mandate (CCP §1085): Failure to Apply Rebuttable Presumption of Helpfulness Under PC §679.10(h)",
        "Count IV — Declaratory Relief (CCP §1060): Facial Deficiency of April 20, 2026 Denial and Construction of PC §679.10"
      ],
      key_facts: "On April 9, 2026, Petitioner submitted a formal request for U Nonimmigrant Status Certification (Form I-918 Supplement B) to CHP pursuant to 8 U.S.C. §1101(a)(15)(U) and California Penal Code §679.10, seeking certification as a victim of qualifying criminal activity. On April 20, 2026, eleven days later, CHP Commander G. Serrato denied the certification in a five-sentence conclusory letter that: (1) identifies no specific statutory element that Petitioner fails to satisfy; (2) does not name a single enumerated qualifying offense considered or rejected; (3) does not engage with CHP's own dashcam recording; (4) does not reconcile the conclusion with CHP's own PC §851.6 detention certificate; and (5) does not acknowledge or apply the statutory rebuttable presumption of helpfulness. Petitioner is a victim of multiple enumerated qualifying offenses under 8 U.S.C. §1101(a)(15)(U)(iii): false imprisonment/unlawful criminal restraint, felonious assault/battery, obstruction of justice, and perjury. Evidence is overwhelming: (1) CHP's own dashcam audio-video recording (November 1, 2024, I-580 East, Livermore) directly contradicts CHP's own sworn arrest report on four material points — the asserted 'strong distinct odor of an alcoholic beverage,' 'slurred' speech, 'very confused and disoriented' demeanor, and 'beverage present: yes'; (2) CHP's own PC §851.6 detention certificate (issued November 5, 2024) reclassifies the arrest as 'a detention only, not an arrest' for 'insufficient grounds for making a criminal complaint'; (3) no criminal charges were ever filed by the Alameda County DA; (4) Petitioner has fully cooperated with every investigative channel — filed citizen complaint, POST complaint, participated in DMV proceedings, obtained and produced the dashcam transcript. The statutory rebuttable presumption of helpfulness (PC §679.10(h)) attaches and is uncontroverted. CHP's April 20, 2026 denial is: (a) based on an incorrect legal standard; (b) made without applying the rebuttable presumption of helpfulness; (c) wholly unsupported by and irreconcilable with the agency's own contemporaneous documentary record; and (d) inconsistent with PC §679.10(k)'s explicit instruction that prosecution and conviction are not required.",
      procedural_status: "Verified Petition for Writ of Mandate filed May 29, 2026, in Alameda County Superior Court (case number TBD; designated for mandatory action under CCP §1085). June 3, 2026: Notice of E-Filing Confirmation received; Petition accepted by court; Notice of Case Management Conference issued; Summons on Petition served on respondents (Serrato, Paul, Dutra, and CHP); Request to Waive Court Fees submitted. Petitioner seeks in Tier 1 (procedural relief): (a) alternative writ directing respondents to show cause; (b) peremptory writ vacating the April 20, 2026 denial; and (c) peremptory writ commanding compliance with reason-giving requirement of PC §679.10(g). In Tier 2 (standards relief): peremptory writ commanding reconsideration under correct legal standards including enumerated offenses, rebuttable presumption, no-prosecution-required rule, and consideration of agency's own evidence. In Tier 3 (substantive relief): conditional certification order if respondents cannot satisfy Tier 2 standards within specified period. Also seeks declaratory relief on statutory interpretation, evidence preservation order, retained jurisdiction for 36 months, and attorney's fees under CCP §1021.5.",
      damages: "Economic: Approximately $112,000 in documented lost wages during one-year CDL suspension (Feb. 20, 2025 – Feb. 19, 2026), with continuing losses at ~$6,000/month. Wage documentation: 26 consecutive weekly Centerline Drivers LLC pay stubs (May 5, 2024 – Jan. 3, 2025) averaging $900–$2,800/week. Non-economic: Severe emotional distress and anxiety from denial of federal immigration relief avenue; complete loss of employment prospects in CDL industry; ongoing professional and reputational harm; out-of-pocket compliance and collateral costs. Total quantifiable damages exceed $112,000 with continuing accrual.",
      risk: "Favorable case with strong documentary support. CHP's own evidence (dashcam recording and detention certificate) defeats the denial on its face. Attorney General's guidance (DLE-2024-05) mandates certification under these facts. Petitioner has clearly satisfied all three statutory elements. The only vulnerability is subject-matter jurisdiction if court deems this a federal U-visa matter — but Petitioner challenges only state-law procedural/substantive duties imposed by the Legislature, not federal adjudication of U-visa eligibility (properly before USCIS).",
      evidence: [
        "CHP Dashcam Video Transcript (November 1, 2024, 00:54:36–01:57:10) — objective contemporaneous record directly contradicting sworn statements in arrest report",
        "CHP Arrest-Investigation Report (CHP Form 202, Case No. RJ68845) — contains four material false sworn statements contradicted by dashcam: 'strong distinct odor of an alcoholic beverage,' 'slurred' speech, 'very confused and disoriented' demeanor, 'beverage present: yes'",
        "PC §851.6 Detention Certificate (Dublin CHP-01991, November 5, 2024) — CHP's own agency reclassifies arrest as 'a detention only, not an arrest' for 'insufficient grounds for making a criminal complaint'",
        "Verified Petition itself (filed May 29, 2026) — detailed pleading with statutory analysis and factual predicate",
        "April 9, 2026 U-Visa Certification Request to CHP — pre-completed Form I-918 Supplement B with 8-page supporting legal/evidentiary memorandum, dashcam transcript, CHP 202, detention certificate, pay stubs, DMV records",
        "April 10, 2026 email from Lt. Gary Paul confirming receipt of certification request ('under review')",
        "April 20, 2026 denial letter from Captain G. Serrato (File No. 390.18437.17620.26-050) — five-sentence conclusory denial",
        "Centerline Drivers LLC pay stubs (26 consecutive weeks, May 5, 2024–Jan. 3, 2025) — wage documentation; $33.35/hr regular, $50.03/hr premium",
        "California DMV temporary license and APS hearing notice (Dec. 4, 2024) — Class A CDL suspension (Feb. 20, 2025–Feb. 19, 2026)",
        "CHP Complaint Assignment Letter (March 26, 2026) — Captain Serrato assigning Sgt. Eric Dutra as investigator",
        "POST Letter of Acknowledgment (March 23, 2026, Case No. PUBLIC-26-000371) — forwarding Petitioner's complaint to CHP",
        "California Attorney General Information Bulletin No. DLE-2024-05 (April 3, 2024) — mandatory guidance on AB 1261 directing all law enforcement to certify when statutory elements are met"
      ],
